'''Remutaka''' (spelled '''Rimutaka''' until 2020) is an [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]] returning one member to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. Since the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 general election]], the seat has been represented by [[Chris Hipkins]], who served as [[Prime Minister of New Zealand]] and is currently the [[Leader of the Opposition]].<ref>[https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/members-of-parliament/hipkins-chris/ New Zealand Parliament – Chris Hipkins MP]</ref>

==Profile==
Centred on [[Upper Hutt]] City, and bisected by [[State Highway 2 (New Zealand)|State Highway 2]] and the [[Hutt River (New Zealand)|Hutt River]], the Remutaka electorate stretches from the [[Lower Hutt]] suburbs of [[Avalon, New Zealand|Avalon]] and [[Naenae]] in the south, via [[Taitā, New Zealand|Taitā]], [[Stokes Valley]], and [[Manor Park, New Zealand|Manor Park]], through [[Silverstream]], [[Trentham, New Zealand|Trentham]], and [[Upper Hutt Central]], to [[Akatarawa Valley|Akatarawa]], [[Te Mārua]], and [[Kaitoke]] in the north.<ref name="Electorate profile">{{cite web |date=6 August 2020 |title=Remutaka electorate profile |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/remutaka-electorate-profile/ |access-date=21 January 2023 |publisher=Parliamentary Library}}{{CC-notice|cc=by4|author=the Parliamentary Library}}</ref>

Of those employed at the [[2018 New Zealand census|2018 census]], 13.7% were clerical and administrative workers (the highest proportion of any general electorate), 11.3% were community and personal service workers (the sixth-highest), and 11.3% worked in the public administration and safety sector (the third-highest share). The main means of travel to work for 12.5% of the employed population of Remutaka was [[Public transport in the Wellington Region|by train]], the third-highest share among general electorates and over six times the New Zealand average (2.0%).<ref name="Electorate profile" />

==History==
Rimutaka was created in {{NZ election link year|1996}} ahead of the change to [[Mixed Member Proportional]] voting. It was created by merging the old Upper Hutt-based seat of {{NZ electorate link|Heretaunga}} with [[Stokes Valley, New Zealand|Stokes Valley]], [[Taitā, New Zealand|Taitā]] and a large section of [[Naenae]] from the defunct {{NZ electorate link|Eastern Hutt}} seat. Eastern Hutt had been held by Labour's [[Paul Swain (politician)|Paul Swain]] since {{NZ election link year|1990}}, while Heretaunga had been won by National's [[Peter McCardle]] in {{NZ election link year|1990}}. Peter McCardle (who had been re-elected in {{NZ election link year|1993}}) defected to [[New Zealand First]] in 1996. Swain was the clear winner in every election from 1996 to 2005; the inclusion of the working-class areas of [[Hutt City]] helped make Rimutaka safer for the Labour Party, though on the campaign trail in 2008, Labour's chances for winning both party vote and the electorate were summarised as: "Labour support is 'rock solid' in the south of the electorate but things are volatile in Upper Hutt, where there is 'still work to do{{'"}}.<ref name="stuff_whatyoutalkingaboutfonz">{{cite web|url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/vote08/4741314a28627.html |title=Labour stronghold shapes up as a battleground |date=28 October 2008|access-date=1 November 2008 |publisher=stuff.co.nz}} {{Dead link|date=October 2010|bot=H3llBot}}</ref>

Following the 2014 boundary review, Rimutaka gained [[Naenae]] and a small part of [[Epuni]] from the {{NZ electorate link|Hutt South}} electorate and lost [[Kelson, New Zealand|Kelson]] and [[Belmont, Wellington|Belmont]] to Hutt South.

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Remutaka.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
Following the 2016 Treaty of Waitangi settlement between the Crown and Rangitāne o Wairarapa and Ngāti Kahungunu ki Wairarapa Tāmaki Nui-ā-Rua, the electorate's namesake Rimutaka Range was renamed to the [[Remutaka Range]]. In the 2019/2020 boundary review, the Representation Commission renamed the electorate Remutaka in line with this name change.<ref>{{cite web |title=What's happening now in the boundary review |url= https://vote.nz/boundary-review-2019-2020/whats-happening-now-in-the-boundary-review/ |access-date=29 January 2020 | publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020 |date=17 April 2020}}</ref>

In the 2025 boundary review, the electorate would expand southwestwards, gaining [[Fairfield, Lower Hutt|Fairfield]] and part of [[Boulcott]] from {{NZ electorate link|Hutt South}}.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=21 September 2025 |page=18 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

